  "response": "**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- P(Disease) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(No Disease) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity = P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity = P(–|No D) = 0.95 → False positive rate P(+|No D) = 0.05\n\n**Compute P(+):**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|No D)·P(No D)\nP(+) = (0.98)(0.002) + (0.05)(0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.04990 = 0.05186\n\n**Apply Bayes' theorem:**\n\nP(D|+) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 = 0.0377941...\n\n**Result:** ≈ 3.78%\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly good test (98% sensitivity, 95% specificity), because the condition is rare, most positive results are false positives — only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually have the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
